# Peter Wiggin · Locke, and the Hegemon of Earth

Peter is the eldest of the three Wiggin children, denied Battle School for being too ruthless. Brilliant and cruel as a boy, he tormented both Ender and Valentine. Yet the ambition that made him dangerous became the engine of his rise: writing under the pseudonym 'Locke,' he manipulates Earth's networks and the aftermath of the war to draw a fractured humanity together, making himself Hegemon and uniting the world under a single rule.

History remembers Peter as a monster. That judgment stands until Ender, having Spoken for the Hive Queen, also writes 'The Hegemon' — a book that tells the true and redeemable story of his brother, recasting the tyrant as a man whose cruelty and greatness were bound up together. In this way the same act of Speaking that gave voice to a slain alien species also restores the human complexity of the boy who once terrorized his own siblings.

Millennia later, in Children of the Mind, Peter returns in a stranger form: Ender's own aiua, unconsciously and without intent, creates a young living copy of his brother. The man condemned by history reappears not as legend but as flesh, an embodied echo of the one who once shaped the fate of Earth.

Peter's emergence is natural: he is an embodied human and a wielder of worldly power, his greatness measured in the politics of nations and the rule of a united Earth rather than in machine, philotic, or spiritual realms. Even his late reappearance arrives as a living, breathing body — power made flesh.
